---
import MainLayout from "../layouts/MainLayout.astro";

const pageTitle = "The Loop — Ethics of Social Media Algorithms";
const description =
  "An informational website exploring how social media algorithms, doomscrolling, and FOMO affect the lives of young adults.";
---

<MainLayout title={pageTitle} description={description}>
  <section class="mx-auto flex w-full max-w-4xl flex-col gap-10 px-4 py-12">
    <article class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-700/80 bg-slate-900/70 px-6 py-8 shadow-lg">
      <div class="mb-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-sky-300">
        <span class="h-px w-6 bg-sky-400/70"></span>
        Introducing "The Loop"
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-100 md:text-xl">
        <p>
          After a long day of college and assignments you come home and decide to sit down
          for a moment to relax.
        </p>
        <p>
          You open your phone “just for a moment, because you need to go cook soon” and head
          over to TikTok to pass a few minutes and catch your breath.
        </p>
        <p>
          TikTok opens straight to a video you are interested in watching:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-6 text-slate-100/90">
          <li>Your favourite music influencer reacting to a new metal song release? <em>"Sounds interesting."</em></li>
          <li>A longer snippet of the same track? <em>"Don’t mind if I do."</em></li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          You didn’t search, you didn’t ask — yet the app simply <em>knew</em> what to show you.
          You were going to listen to that song later in the day anyway.
        </p>
        <p>
          That feeling of “wow, how did it know I was going to watch this?” is your first clue
          that there is a powerful prediction engine sitting between you and almost everything
          you see online.
        </p>
        <p>
          That engine is the <strong>recommendation algorithm</strong>. And once you understand how
          it works, a lot of things about social media suddenly start making sense.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="max-w-prose space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-200 md:text-lg">
      <p>
        Early social networks were basically just digital newspaper subscriptions. You followed
        people and you saw their posts in time order: newest at the top and older ones below.
        In the industry, they call this the <strong>reverse chronological feed</strong>.
      </p>

      <p>
        But as platforms grew in user base, that model broke down. There was simply too much content.
        If you follow hundreds of accounts and each of them posts at least once a day, most of what
        they share will sink without you ever getting a chance to see it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Modern platforms don’t treat the feed as a simple list anymore. They treat it as a
        <strong> competition for your attention</strong>.
      </p>

      <p class="border-l-2 border-sky-500/60 pl-4 italic text-slate-100/90">
        “Out of all the possible things we could show this person right now, which posts are most
        likely to keep them interested?” 
      </p>

      <p>
        — that’s the question the system asks itself every time
        you open the app.<br>
        Long gone are the days where your feed simply showed “what’s out there”. What you see now
        is precisely what the algorithm has selected and ordered <em>for you</em>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl space-y-12 leading-relaxed">

  <!-- Information flow -->
  <article class="max-w-prose space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-200 md:text-lg">
    <div class="mb-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-sky-300">
      <span class="h-px w-6 bg-sky-400/70"></span>
      How information reaches your feed
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-100 md:text-xl">
      <p>
        The information flow breaks down into three basic models
        (<em>Narayanan, 2023</em>). In reality, platforms blend them all together,
        however the distinction helps explain what’s going on behind the scenes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Subscription -->
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-700/80 bg-slate-900/70 px-6 py-8 shadow-lg">
    <div class="mb-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-sky-300">
      <span class="h-px w-6 bg-sky-400/70"></span>
      Subscription
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-100 md:text-xl">
      <p>
        You follow or subscribe to accounts you care about, this includes relatives,
        friends, influencers, artists, etc.
      </p>

      <div class="flex justify-center text-slate-300">
        ↓
      </div>

      <p>
        Your feed shows posts from those accounts, often in the previously mentioned format
        (latest posts first).
      </p>

      <p>
        This is the most straightforward model – what you subscribe to is what you get,
        that being your friends’ posts, your favourite artists’ new music releases and
        recent news from channels you follow.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Network -->
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-700/80 bg-slate-900/70 px-6 py-8 shadow-lg">
    <div class="mb-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-sky-300">
      <span class="h-px w-6 bg-sky-400/70"></span>
      Network
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-100 md:text-xl">
      <p>
        Realistically, you don’t see just what your friends post, but also what they
        <strong>share</strong>. Posts can travel very far this way, from a friend → friend
        of a friend → an absolute stranger in a methodical chain.
      </p>

      <p>
        As an example, picture the classic “viral tweet”, where a certain post due to its
        reposts and shares escapes its original audience and spreads, often even towards
        audiences not familiar with the topic.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Algorithmic -->
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-700/80 bg-slate-900/70 px-6 py-8 shadow-lg">
    <div class="mb-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-sky-300">
      <span class="h-px w-6 bg-sky-400/70"></span>
      Algorithmic
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-100 md:text-xl">
      <p>
        In this model, who you follow and what you are subscribed to is almost optional.
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-slate-100/90">
        <li>The platform looks at your past post interaction behaviour</li>
        <li>It looks at the behaviour of millions of other users</li>
        <li>
          It does its best to predict what post you are most likely to interact with,
          and shows it – regardless if you follow the post author or whether you are
          previously interested in the topic
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The most typical example of this is TikTok’s <strong>For You</strong> page: most
        of what you see comes from accounts you have never even heard of, chosen simply
        because users with similar behaviours engaged with them.
      </p>

      <p>
        Most big social media apps take a hybrid approach, leveraging the advantages of
        each of these models.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Algorithm goal -->
  <article class="max-w-prose space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-200 md:text-lg">
    <div class="mb-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-sky-300">
      <span class="h-px w-6 bg-sky-400/70"></span>
      What is the algorithm’s main goal?
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-100 md:text-xl">
      <p>
        The answer to this question often varies, as different platforms have different goals.
        Some apps simply want to make money, some want to keep users happy enough to come back,
        or even grow the user base further.
      </p>

      <p>
        However, there is no direct way to say:
      </p>

      <blockquote class="border-l-2 border-sky-500/70 pl-4 italic text-slate-100/90">
        “Serving <em>these</em> posts to <em>these</em> users will increase revenue in six months.”
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Therefore the system commonly uses an alternate goal:
        <strong>engagement</strong>.
      </p>

      <blockquote class="border-l-2 border-slate-600 pl-4 italic text-slate-100/90">
        “If I show the user this post, what is the chance they will interact with it in any way”
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        The idea is simple: if you’re engaging, you’re staying, and if you’re staying,
        you’re more likely to see ads and return tomorrow.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Ethical concerns -->
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-700/80 bg-slate-900/70 px-6 py-8 shadow-lg">
    <div class="mb-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-sky-300">
      <span class="h-px w-6 bg-sky-400/70"></span>
      Ethical concerns
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-100 md:text-xl">

      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h4 class="font-semibold">Manipulation</h4>

        <p>
          Because the feed is algorithmically curated, users do not simply choose content —
          content is <em>chosen for them</em>. This creates conditions for
          <strong>manipulation</strong>.
        </p>

        <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-slate-100/90">
          <li>emotionally charged content is prioritised</li>
          <li>fantastic or dramatic posts receive more visibility</li>
          <li>users are nudged toward certain topics, beliefs, or behaviours</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          Platforms deliberately amplify content that elicits
          <em>strong emotional responses</em> because it prolongs engagement, even if it
          harms psychological wellbeing (<em>Nosraty, 2021</em>).
        </p>

        <p>
          Young adults — currently navigating identity development — are particularly
          vulnerable to such influence.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h4 class="font-semibold">Data extraction</h4>

        <p>
          In order to function, recommendation systems rely on massive amounts of user data.
          This includes:
        </p>

        <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-slate-100/90">
          <li>every like, share, and follow</li>
          <li>time spent on each post</li>
          <li>where users pause while scrolling</li>
          <li>location data</li>
          <li>interaction networks</li>
          <li>device and behavioural metadata</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          Narayanan describes behavioural data as
          “the fuel of the recommendation algorithm’s engine” — without this almost
          surveillance-like data collection, personalised feeds would not really work
          (<em>Narayanan, 2023</em>).
        </p>

        <p>
          Privacy is then not just about data leaking, but about the ongoing extraction of
          behavioural data to refine algorithmic prediction and monetisation.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </article>

</section>

</MainLayout>